<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stephen II, Duke of Bavaria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1319–1375</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">House of Wittelsbach</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stephen II, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, was a son of Emperor Louis IV the Bavarian who ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-bavaria">Lower Bavaria</a> and from 1363 also <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-bavaria">Upper Bavaria</a>. He reunited much of the Wittelsbach duchy and acquired claims to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a>. In 1392 his three sons divided Bavaria into the duchies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria-landshut">Bavaria-Landshut</a>, Bavaria-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> and Bavaria-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingolstadt">Ingolstadt</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stephen-ii-duke-of-bavaria.jpg" alt="Stephen II, Duke of Bavaria" width="320" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Julius Zimmermann · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Julius_Zimmermann_-_Stephan_II._mit_der_Hafte_(geb._1313,_regierte_1347-1375),_Herzog_von_Bayern_-_4544_-_Bavarian_State_Painting_Collections.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Stephen II was the second son of Emperor Louis IV the Bavarian and his first wife, Beatrice of Silesia, and a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">Wittelsbach dynasty</a>. During his father's reign he served as vogt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a>. Louis IV had acquired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a>, Holland and Hainaut for his house and had reunited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> in 1340. On 27 June 1328 Stephen married Elisabetta of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, daughter of King Frederick III of Sicily, and all his children came from this marriage; in 1359 he married Margarete of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When his father died in 1347, Stephen succeeded as Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and Count of Holland and Hainaut together with his five brothers. In 1349 the country was divided again, and Stephen ruled Holland and Lower Bavaria-Landshut with his brothers William I and Albert I until 1353, and afterwards Lower Bavaria-Landshut alone. After a reconciliation with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iv">Emperor Charles IV</a> he joined the emperor's expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1354, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1356">Golden Bull of 1356</a> caused a new conflict, as only the Palatinate branch and his brother Louis VI the Roman received the electoral dignity. In 1362 he was absolved from excommunication.</p><p>When Duke Meinhard, son of his elder brother Louis V the Brandenburger, died in 1363, Stephen succeeded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-bavaria">Upper Bavaria</a> and invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a>, allying himself with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernabo-visconti">Bernabò Visconti</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-iv-duke-of-austria">Rudolf IV, Duke of Austria</a>. After the death of Margarete Maultasch in 1369 he gave up Tyrol to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> in the Peace of Schärding for a large payment. His quarrel with Louis VI over Meinhard's inheritance led Louis to make <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iv">Charles IV</a> his heir, which in the end cost the Wittelsbach dynasty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Stephen died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landshut">Landshut</a> on 13 May 1375 and is buried in the Frauenkirche in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>. His three sons, Stephen III, Frederick and John II, succeeded him; two of them, Stephen III and Frederick, married daughters of his ally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernabo-visconti">Bernabò Visconti</a>. His daughter Agnes married King James I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Stephen II of Bavaria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1392 the three sons of <b>Duke Stephen II</b> divided Bavaria: Frederick took Bavaria-Landshut, John II the Duchy of Bavaria-Munich and Stephen III the Duchy of Bavaria-Ingolstadt.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-bavaria-landshut">Duchy of Bavaria-Landshut</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landshut">Landshut</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.14,11.58&amp;year=1355" aria-label="Munich on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4819 479 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Stephen II, Duke of Bavaria"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5269 749h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5269 749h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.14,11.58&amp;year=1355">What was here in 1355? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
